## Fig26

The final 640 x 1024 image has two vertically arranged, independently framed panels. Both retain 18 by 12 half-unit cells. The upper panel has the gently rising solid separator through the supported three nodes and upper-left descending hatch. Its two dashed horizontal coordinate projections remain distinct from the separator. The lower panel has the steep solid separator through the supported half-unit positive x crossing and negative-unit y crossing, with right/below descending hatch.

The lower-panel origin `0` is now southwest of the origin and clear of the steep separator. The other visible labels and positive-axis arrowheads fit inside the canvas. Neither panel shows equations, inequalities, ordered pairs, radical values, or named endpoint markers. The negative-level guide fragment's regularized extent remains a declared modern addition, not recovered source endpoint data.

## Fig27

The upper panel has an origin-centered radius-2 outline, both internal vertical cap segments, and two disconnected lateral hatched caps. The central strip and the exterior are unhatched. The lower panel has the common unit-height junction, both x-axis intersections, all four diagonal continuations, and only the left-facing wedge hatched. The relocated upper `1` is visually separated from the junction and diagonal strokes.

**Concern for the fresh independent reviewer:** pale lattice strokes visibly cross the circular rim, producing local light interruptions/overpainting at some grid intersections. The spec's circle is constructed as a complete solid object and the mathematical contacts are asserted; this raster-layer appearance still needs source-versus-modern visual judgment against the newly supported continuous-arc fact. Renderer exit 0 and empty collision lists do not settle this source-fidelity question. This author issues no whole-circle or figure approval and does not claim this concern resolved.

